[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Conflicts are intensifying over the push to merge Jeonju and Wanju in Jeonbuk Province. After more than 30 years of repeated failures, the merger discussion has now ignited through a residents&rsquo; referendum. Proponents argue it will strengthen regional competitiveness and create a metropolitan economic zone, while opponents fear damage to local identity and unfair development.

On the 25th of last month, ahead of Jeonbuk Governor Kim Kwan-young&rsquo;s visit, hundreds of Wanju residents gathered in front of the county office chanting &ldquo;No Merger,&rdquo; and 10 members of the Wanju County Council shaved their heads in protest. Residents said, &ldquo;Only undesirable facilities like waste incineration plants will come in,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;We will never agree to the merger.&rdquo; Seo Nam-yong, head of the Merger Opposition Special Committee, said, &ldquo;Even if it&rsquo;s more than a referendum, it will definitely be rejected.&rdquo;

Meanwhile, the Wanju County Residents Council and Jeonju Citizens Council agreed on 105 co-prosperity measures, proposing to build the integrated city hall in Wanju, ban relocation of unwanted facilities, and place the agricultural bureau in Wanju. Na Yoo-in, co-representative of the Wanju County Residents Council, said, &ldquo;This merger is a historical restoration to reverse the division imposed by Japanese colonial rule,&rdquo; emphasizing that &ldquo;it is both the first step and the last chance for Jeonbuk&rsquo;s development.&rdquo;

Governor Kim Kwan-young and Jeonju Mayor Woo Beom-ki stated that the merger is &ldquo;a task for Jeonbuk&rsquo;s future&rdquo; and expressed their intention to respect residents&rsquo; opinions. However, Wanju County Mayor Yoo Hee-tae and the county council countered that &ldquo;no form of merger without residents&rsquo; consent can ever be accepted.&rdquo;

Amid divided public opinion, the Jeonju-Wanju merger stands at a critical crossroads that will determine Jeonbuk&rsquo;s future.

