[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Efforts to merge Jeonju and Wanju in Jeonbuk are intensifying conflicts among residents, local governments, and civic groups. The merger debate, which has repeatedly failed for over 30 years, has now reignited with a resident vote. Proponents emphasize enhanced regional competitiveness and economic synergy, while opponents fear damage to local identity, unfair development, and a lack of democratic legitimacy. As tensions deepen, Jeonbuk stands at a crossroads: will the merger lead to co-growth, or will it leave only new scars?

&ldquo;Why merge when we&rsquo;re living just fine now.&rdquo;

On June 25, ahead of Jeonbuk Governor Kim Kwan-young&rsquo;s visit, the plaza in front of Wanju County Office was tense from early morning. Hundreds of county residents gathered before large speakers, shouting &ldquo;No to merger&rdquo; and banging gongs, as 10 Wanju County Council members shaved their heads in protest.

◆ &ldquo;Merger only harms us&rdquo;

The scene in front of the county office was like a battlefield. Hundreds of residents beat gongs and shouted &ldquo;No to merger.&rdquo; People wore red headbands, held placards, clashed with police, and shouted slogans until their voices cracked. Their faces showed desperation to &lsquo;protect Wanju.&rsquo;

Seo Nam-yong, head of the Wanju-Jeonju Anti-Merger Special Committee, criticized, &ldquo;Governor Kim Kwan-young is pushing for the merger despite fierce opposition from Wanju residents due to his political ambitions.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;Even if it&rsquo;s more than a resident vote, it will be overwhelmingly rejected,&rdquo; arguing, &ldquo;Hiding the results of nine surveys proves how strong our opposition is.&rdquo;

Assemblywoman Lee Kyung-ae declared, &ldquo;I shaved my head out of love for Wanju. I will protect Wanju with you.&rdquo; Assemblyman Shim Bu-geon shouted, &ldquo;Governor Kim alone cannot take Wanju from us. We will protect Wanju at all costs.&rdquo; Assemblyman Sung Joong-gi urged, &ldquo;If we don&rsquo;t protect Wanju, who will? Let&rsquo;s show our strength today and remain united.&rdquo;

Residents adjusted their headbands and unanimously said, &ldquo;We don&rsquo;t need a merger.&rdquo; Yoo Jeom-sun (alias, 65) said, &ldquo;We&rsquo;re living fine, and nothing good will come from merging,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;We&rsquo;ll just end up with incinerators and other unwanted facilities, and if we merge with debt-ridden Jeonju, only we will lose.&rdquo; She emphasized, &ldquo;We will never merge. Just let us live as we are.&rdquo;

Lee Soon-ja (in her 60s) said, &ldquo;I came out to protect our county and our descendants,&rdquo; questioning, &ldquo;When Iksan merged, what good did it bring? Our villages will be filled with unwanted facilities.&rdquo;

Lee Don-seung, head of the Kim Dae-jung Foundation&rsquo;s Wanju branch (66, male), said, &ldquo;They&rsquo;re here to persuade us. This isn&rsquo;t a dialogue,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;We&rsquo;re protesting because we fundamentally need to block this.&rdquo; He criticized, &ldquo;The governor should act as mediator and coordinator, but leading the merger is just political greed.&rdquo; He pointed out, &ldquo;Jeonju has 600 billion won in debt, which increased by 200 billion in just a year. How can people who can&rsquo;t manage their own debt help us?&rdquo; He concluded, &ldquo;Wanju has surpassed a population of 100,000 and is a blue ocean. Merging with Jeonju is disrespectful to us. The merger will only cause division among residents and should be stopped immediately.&rdquo;

Governor Kim&rsquo;s scheduled town hall meeting with residents was ultimately cancelled that day. Even as he left in his vehicle after a press briefing, protests by residents and county council members continued. Protesters surrounded his car, holding placards reading &ldquo;Protect Wanju! No to merger!&rdquo; Police were dispatched to clear the scene before the vehicle could leave.

Song Byung-joo, senior representative of the Wanju-Jeonju Anti-Merger Countermeasure Committee, said, &ldquo;Governor Kim backing down is a small victory, but this isn&rsquo;t over,&rdquo; urging, &ldquo;Please tell your family and neighbors to vote against the merger so we can win the resident vote.&rdquo;


