◆ &ldquo;Merger is for Jeonbuk&rsquo;s future&rdquo;

Governor Kim Kwan-young and Jeonju Mayor Woo Beom-ki defined the merger as a task for Jeonbuk&rsquo;s future and the last chance to counter regional extinction, vowing to respect residents&rsquo; will.

At his 3rd-anniversary press conference, Governor Kim said, &ldquo;Jeonbuk is facing new opportunities, and the Jeonju-Wanju merger is an unfinished task we must resolve,&rdquo; emphasizing, &ldquo;With the bid to host the 2036 Jeonju Olympics, the passage of the Great Metropolitan Transport Act, and the launch of a new government, the merger has become even more necessary.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;We must ask and answer together what future Jeonju and Wanju will choose. I will prioritize residents&rsquo; will and communicate with empathy.&rdquo;

Regarding the recent failed town hall meeting, he explained, &ldquo;Going forward, we will create more natural communication opportunities through small group meetings.&rdquo;

Mayor Woo Beom-ki also emphasized at his 3rd-anniversary press conference, &ldquo;Jeonju and Wanju must become one again,&rdquo; calling it &ldquo;Jeonju&rsquo;s survival issue amid the massive wave of regional extinction and the last chance to become a metropolitan city.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;The most important thing is communication and understanding,&rdquo; promising sincere dialogue with Wanju residents in cooperation with civic groups.

Regarding the Wanju County Council&rsquo;s proposal for a televised debate, he said, &ldquo;Residents&rsquo; will is most important. They need thorough explanations and discussions to make a calm judgment,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;The method and timing will be decided in consultation with the council speaker.&rdquo;

Mayor Woo later held another press conference on July 7, pledging, &ldquo;I will communicate with Wanju residents anytime,&rdquo; and declared Jeonju&rsquo;s active acceptance of the 105 co-prosperity measures jointly proposed by the councils.

The city plans to categorize the 105 projects into short- and long-term actions, establish implementation plans, and secure funding for projects it can pursue independently. For projects requiring other agencies or the private sector, such as adjusting school districts or attracting large enterprises, hospitals, and commercial facilities, it will continue discussions to ensure smooth progress.

Mayor Woo said, &ldquo;We have taken another crucial step towards a merger that will completely transform our region&rsquo;s future,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;This is a different beginning and step because it is a people-driven merger where Wanju residents proposed co-prosperity plans and both residents and citizens are coming together to challenge themselves.&rdquo; He concluded, &ldquo;If there are any disadvantages, we will find solutions, and if there are misunderstandings, we will resolve them through dialogue. Jeonju will prioritize residents&rsquo; will and pursue a people-driven merger to the very end.&rdquo;


