◆ &ldquo;First and last chance for Jeonbuk&rsquo;s development&rdquo;

Na Yoo-in, co-chair of Wanju Residents&rsquo; Council, told Cheonji Ilbo in a recent call that the merger push &ldquo;aims to restore Jeonju and Wanju to their original state after being divided by Japan,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;Unlike previous government-led efforts, this time residents directly initiated the resident vote.&rdquo;

Na explained, &ldquo;In 1935, Japan divided Jeonju and Wanju. We formed a Historical Restoration Committee to reverse this. In the past, governors, mayors, and county heads made merger pledges but achieved nothing. We shifted to a resident-led movement because a resident vote must be held two years before the election for the merged city mayor.&rdquo;

He cited past mergers such as Cheongju-Cheongwon and Gwangju-Gwangsan as examples, arguing, &ldquo;The global trend is toward metropolitan cities. Merging Jeonju and Wanju will greatly benefit enterprise attraction, education competitiveness, transportation expansion, agriculture, and welfare.&rdquo;

Regarding the 105 co-prosperity measures, he said, &ldquo;They include bans on relocating unwanted facilities, moving the merged city hall to Wanju, maintaining and expanding welfare, and guaranteeing personnel rights for public servants,&rdquo; and emphasized, &ldquo;Like Cheongju-Cheongwon, a joint monitoring body must be established to continuously check implementation.&rdquo;

He stressed, &ldquo;To ensure feasibility, specific budgets, implementation timelines, and legal and ordinance changes must be arranged,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;If this merger fails, Jeonbuk has no future. The Jeonju-Wanju merger is both the first step and the last chance for Jeonbuk&rsquo;s development.&rdquo;

The revived merger debate pits calls to restore historical borders against cries to protect cherished hometowns. Whether this merger will become a path of &lsquo;growing together&rsquo; or leave deeper wounds dividing the community remains uncertain. Uniting divided public opinion is the starting point for the merger discussion &ndash; and Jeonbuk&rsquo;s greatest challenge.

